1. Need some experiments to figure out the reason when the output is huge,
   nvim will get frozen temporarily, especially when using gnu-grep.
   e.g. Searching GPL or struct in the linux kernel codebase(pressure tests)

   The reason I guessed is processing the large amount of data takes a lot of
   time to finish. And the processing happens in the nvim's main loop that will
   cause nvim can't handle any other things, such as user's keystrokes, rerendering
   the screen, etc.

   If that is really the reason(need experiments to verify), then the solution is below:
   Instead of processing the data on the fly, batching the data into a buffer first,
   which is a table in lua, every time I need to process them, only processing
   a limited number(configurable by user) of them not all of them, using `timer`
   or `coroutine with defer_fn` to schedule the processing.
   The downside of this method is it will slow down the whole searching time
   a little bit(or maybe not just a little bit). But the user experience should be
   improved.

   ```lua
   -- Configurable options:
   {
     batch = {
       enable = true,
       process_items = 1000, -- every time it be scheduled, the maximum items it will process.
       intervals = 50, -- how long it should be rescheduled. unit: ms
     }
   }
   ```

2. Need to listen one of nvim quit events for clearing the search process to prevent freezing nvim sometimes
  - QuitPre, ExitPre, VimLeavePre
